  "worked_solution": "We're given that there exists a point on the line where $y=0.$ Substituting this to the first equation gives\n\n$$\n2x-0-6=0\\,\u27f9\\,x=3,\n$$\n\nand substituting $y=0$ into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n0+z+6=0\\,\u27f9\\,z=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $a=\u27e83,0,-6\u27e9$ is a position vector of a point on the line.\n\nNext, we notice:\n\n- For the plane $2x-y-6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{1}=\u27e82,-1,0\u27e9.$\n\n- For the plane $y+z+6=0,$ a normal vector to the plane is ${n}_{2}=\u27e80,1,1\u27e9.$\n\nTo find a direction vector for the line, we calculate the cross product of these normal vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}b & ={n}_{1}\\times {n}_{2} \\\\  & =\u27e82,-1,0\u27e9\\times \u27e80,1,1\u27e9 \\\\  & =\\left|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1\\end{aligned}\\right| \\\\  & =-i-2j+2k \\\\  & =\u27e8-1,-2,2\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation of the line is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =a+tb \\\\ r(t) & =\u27e83,0,-6\u27e9+t\u27e8-1,-2,2\u27e9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, ${a}_{1}+{a}_{3}=3-6=-3,$ and $b=\u27e8-1,-2,2\u27e9.$",
